==English==

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|sa|नमस्कार}}, from {{af|sa|नमस्|t1=bow, [[obeisance]]|कार|t2=action|nocat=1}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/naməˈskɑː/|a=UK}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|India}} The use of the [[greet]]ing in which one puts one's hands together and [[bow]]s slightly; greeting with a [[namaste]]. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|work=The General Baptist repository, and Missionary observer|page=286|year=1847|passage=To every one I met I made my '''namaskar''', and on entering my village, I made '''namaskar''' to a bowri, (a man of low caste.)|title=Letter from Mr. Stubbins}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1997|author=w:Kiran Nagarkar|title=Cuckold|page=228|publisher=HarperCollins|year_published=2013
|passage=I bowed down, did '''namaskar''' and said a prayer.}}
#* '''2015''', Tridip Suhrud, translating {{w|Govardhanram Madhavram Tripathi}}, ''Sarasvatichandra'' I, Orient BlackSwan 2015, p. 83:
#*: Navinchandra greeted the Counsellor with a '''namaskar'''; Buddhidhan responded solemnly.

